  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nCurrently, the ridge is centered over the Southeastern US with the \nridge axis extending northward into the Great Lakes. Broad troughing \nis digging into the northern US from the Canadian Prairies. A \nsurface front extends generally along Hwy 50, though the mid-level \nbaroclinic zone is located further north along I-70. It is in this \ncorridor that we're also seeing anomalously high PWATs. As the \nLLJ increased overnight, storms fired along the nose of the jet \nand quickly formed into a bowing MCS that propagated east-\nnortheast along the mid-level baroclinic zone. As of 245AM, this\nsegment is pushing out of the forecast area with stratiform \nrain and lightning lingering in its wake. This stratiform \nactivity will linger across central Kansas through daybreak \nbefore diminishing and pushing east out of the region. \n\nThrough the rest of today, as an upper level trough continues to \ntraverse the Canadian Prairies and the northern tier of the US, the \nridge will be squashed further south. The stationary boundary that \nhas been draped across the state is finally pushing southward across \nthe state as a cold front. Early this morning, winds have turned \nnorthward across much of central Kansas, but the widespread \"cooler\" \ntemperatures will lag behind the boundary slightly. Central and \nportions of south-central Kansas can expect more seasonable \ntemperatures (upper 80s, lower 90s) this afternoon, but \ntemperatures in the upper 90s to around 100 will linger for the \nfar southern counties in the forecast area. A few showers or \nstorms are possible across central Kansas this afternoon along a\nremnant outflow boundary from the overnight MCS, widespread \nconvection is not anticipated. Then, just behind the front in \nsouthern Kansas later tonight into Monday morning, moisture will\nlinger allowing for a few isolated showers and sub-severe \nstorms to develop near the frontal boundary. Drier air will \nbegin to filter into the region later on Monday with widespread \nupper 80s and 90s for the forecast area on Monday afternoon.\n\nThen by Tuesday, the trough will dig into the Great Lakes region and \nthe northeastern tier of the US and ridging will begin to build back \nin over the Plains from the west. We will see temperatures respond \naccordingly with upper 90s and lower 100s expected areawide on \nTuesday. The widespread heat will be short-lived as a shortwave will \nsweep across the Plains on Wednesday. The placement of the \ncorresponding surface boundary will lead to cooler temperatures \nWednesday afternoon across central Kansas, with 100s across southern \nportions of the state. POPs increase for the region on Wednesday \nnight with the approach/passage of the front. \n\nSeasonably mild temperatures behind the front, along with a drier \nair mass, are then expected for Thu/Fri. For the tail end of the \nforecast period, models are suggesting we may see the ridge try to \nbuild back in over the region from the west. Currently maintaining \nseasonably warm temperatures for day 7 (Saturday), but this \ntrend could lead to more widespread heat beyond the forecast \ntime frame.",
